Short answer:

Yes, many of them should be tuned from data, but

No, they should not all be continuously flailing around in real time.

Think of your parameters in three layers:

Layer A – Structural constants (rarely change)
These mostly define what kind of problem you’re solving:

msmr_bar_interval_sec (1s vs 2s vs 5s)

msmr_window_length_sec (30–90s scale)

You can choose these based on:

Latency budget

Strategy time horizon

Human intuition about microstructure timescales

I’d keep these fixed for a long time (weeks/months) and only revisit them when you fundamentally change strategy granularity.

Layer B – Thresholds & filters (tune offline, update slowly)
Good candidates for data-driven tuning:

msmr_min_window_volatility

msmr_trend_zscore_threshold

msmr_min_up_bar_fraction, msmr_min_down_bar_fraction

msmr_min_segment_duration_sec

How to tune:

Offline on historical data, do simple sweeps / grid search:

E.g.:

trend z-score ∈ {0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.5}

min_up/down_frac ∈ {0.6, 0.65, 0.7}

For each combo, compute:

how often each regime occurs

how well your momentum toy strategy performs inside low-entropy / trend regimes

Keep the set that:

Gives “clean” regimes (not too rare, not everywhere)

Maximizes Sharpe / information ratio of your entropy-gated momentum strategy.

Update frequency:

Every few weeks or months, or when you deploy to a new symbol / exchange.

You could optimize these with a GA/PSO “genotype”, but I’d still treat the output as slow-moving config, not something that changes every hour.

Layer C – Smoothing & regime hysteresis (tune and adapt slowly)
These:

msmr_score_ema_halflife_sec

msmr_low_regime_enter_threshold, msmr_low_regime_exit_threshold

msmr_high_regime_enter_threshold, msmr_high_regime_exit_threshold

msmr_min_regime_dwell_time_sec

are mostly about stability vs reactivity.

You can:

Tune them offline by looking at:

Regime duration distribution

Number of switches/day

PnL impact of being more/less reactive.

Possibly adjust them per market:

Hyper-volatile small caps → maybe shorter halflife and shorter dwell

BTC → more stable, longer halflife.

I’d not let these drift continuously from online PnL; instead:

Re-estimate / retune them on rolling windows (e.g. last 1–3 months)

Commit new values as config when you deploy a new model version.

What should adapt continuously?
Two things:

The regime score model itself (e.g., your SVM or linear model mapping entropy → regime).

You can retrain / refit this periodically as microstructure changes.

Output is still plugged into the same MSMR thresholds.

The trading strategy on top (position sizing, alpha models, RL policy)

These can be more dynamic, but still ideally updated discretely (new model versions) rather than every minute.

Why not constantly re-learn all MSMR parameters online?
Risk of overfitting to short-term noise (especially in whippy crypto).

You lose interpretability: you want to know what “trend regime” mathematically means.

Debugging becomes impossible if both regime labels and trading logic are moving targets.

Much better pattern:

Start with sensible defaults (like the ones above).

Tune them offline with simple data-driven methods.

Lock them into config for at least some period (days/weeks).

Only periodically re-run calibration as a controlled process.

